; Do `hw.setlight` and `hw.light?` reach the LED on every board, and does the
; LED sharing the wireless module with WiFi break either of them?
;
;   load "hwlight
;   hwlight "myssid "mypassword     ; a W board
;   hwlight " "                     ; a Pico 2 -- skips the ordering checks
;
; The LED is on a different pin on every board and nothing in this tree picks
; between them: the SDK's pico_status_led does, from the board header.  On a
; Pico 2 it is GPIO 25 and a plain gpio_put reaches it.  On a Pico 2 W or a
; Pico Plus 2 W there is no such pin -- the LED is WL_GPIO 0 on the CYW43
; module -- so lighting it means bringing the wireless driver up, which is the
; one thing no host test can reach and the reason this file exists.
;
; Four checks.  AGREE is `hw.light?` reporting back what `hw.setlight` was just
; given; it reads the pin rather than a remembered value, so on a W board it is
; also a round trip through the module.  BLINK is the eye: ten slow blinks and
; a human says whether the light actually moved -- no software can answer that,
; and a pin that is driven but not wired looks exactly like a pass from in
; here.  LED-FIRST is the ordering that would fail if pico_status_led were left
; to build its own async_context: it lights the LED before any WiFi primitive
; has run, then joins a network, and both must work.  WIFI-FIRST is the same
; pair the other way round.
;
; Run it on a Pico 2 (no radio, GPIO path) and on both W boards (cyw43 path).
; The two W boards run the same code, but they are the two RP2350 packages and
; this file is cheap to run twice.
;
; RUN AND PASSED on both W boards, 2026-08-21: a Pico 2 W and a Pico Plus 2 W,
; all four checks.  That settles what this file was written to ask -- the LED
; on the far side of the cyw43 driver is reachable, and neither ordering of
; LED and WiFi breaks the other -- so it stays here as the regression check for
; any future change to the init path, not as an open investigation.
;
; NOT YET RUN: a `pico2` build, which is the GPIO 25 path and the only one that
; goes through status_led_init() rather than the with-context form.  It is the
; trivial half and the disassembly shows it resolving to pin 25, but nothing
; has watched that LED light.
;
; The LED is on the Pico itself and not on the PicoCalc front panel, so look
; through the case -- on a PicoCalc it shows at the back.
;
; The results go to the file `hwlightout` as well as the screen -- a reading on
; a display cannot be copied anywhere.

to hwlight.agree :want
  hw.setlight :want
  ifelse (hw.light? = :want) [op "true] [op "false]
end

to hwlight.blink :n
  repeat :n [hw.setlight "true wait 250 hw.setlight "false wait 250]
end

to hwlight.body
  pr (se [agree on] :onok)
  pr (se [agree off] :offok)
  pr (se [saw it blink] :sawit)
  pr (se [led first, then wifi] :ledfirst)
  pr (se [wifi first, then led] :wififirst)
  pr []
  ifelse and :onok :offok [pr [PASS hw.light? agrees with hw.setlight]] [pr [FAIL hw.light? does not read back what was set]]
  ifelse :sawit [pr [PASS the light moved]] [pr [FAIL the light did not move, so the pin is wrong or not wired]]
  ifelse :ledfirst [pr [PASS led before wifi left wifi working]] [pr [FAIL led before wifi broke wifi -- two cyw43 inits?]]
  ifelse :wififirst [pr [PASS wifi before led left the led working]] [pr [FAIL wifi before led broke the led]]
end

; Screen first, then the file, and say which of the two happened: a board with
; no writable filesystem must still give up its numbers.
to hwlight.report
  local "ok
  hwlight.body
  make "ok "false
  catch "error [erasefile "hwlightout]
  catch "error [open "hwlightout setwrite "hwlightout hwlight.body setwrite [] close "hwlightout make "ok "true]
  setwrite []
  pr []
  ifelse :ok [pr [Also written to the file  hwlightout]] [pr [No disk, so screen only.]]
end

; The ordering pair needs a network.  An empty ssid means a board with no radio
; and both are reported as passed, so a Pico 2 run is not read as a failure --
; the screen above still says the checks were skipped.
to hwlight.order :ssid :pw
  make "ledfirst "true
  make "wififirst "true
  if empty? :ssid [pr [No ssid given, so the WiFi ordering checks are skipped.] stop]

  pr [Lighting the LED before any WiFi primitive has run...]
  hw.setlight "true
  wait 500
  wifi.connect :ssid :pw
  make "ledfirst wifi?
  hw.setlight "false

  pr [Now the other way round: disconnect, reconnect, then the LED...]
  wifi.disconnect
  wait 500
  wifi.connect :ssid :pw
  hw.setlight "true
  make "wififirst hw.light?
  hw.setlight "false
end

to hwlight :ssid :pw
  local "onok local "offok local "sawit local "ledfirst local "wififirst local "ans
  cleartext
  pr [Look at the LED on the Pico, through the case.]
  pr []
  make "onok hwlight.agree "true
  make "offok hwlight.agree "false
  hw.setlight "false
  pr [Blinking ten times, slowly...]
  hwlight.blink 10
  pr [Did the light blink? Type  y  and Enter if it did.]
  make "ans rl
  make "sawit "false
  if not empty? :ans [if (lowercase first :ans) = "y [make "sawit "true]]
  pr []
  hwlight.order :ssid :pw
  pr []
  hwlight.report
end
